# DrawIR v3 execution-route selection (CPU-reference / wave 1).
#
# Source of truth:
#   doc/03_plan/platform/structural_compute/webrender_gpu_offload_plan.md
#     "Variable execution config" — cpu only / compatibility / balanced /
#     full offload, selected per session, "no rebuild, no silent downgrade
#     (`cpu_selected` by cost policy != `gpu_fallback`)".
#   doc/03_plan/platform/structural_compute/README.md shared rules 3, 4, 5.
#   doc/03_plan/ui/gpu_web_scene_offload_mdsoc_plus_plan.md §7
#     "Avoiding accidental fallback" rules 7 and 9.
#
# THE INVARIANT THIS FILE EXISTS TO ENFORCE
# -----------------------------------------
# A DrawIR v3 submission that does not run on the GPU took one of two
# categorically different paths, and a receipt must always say WHICH:
#
#   cpu_selected  the policy CHOSE the CPU. Nothing failed. Not a fallback.
#   gpu_fallback  the GPU was DENIED the work. Something failed or was
#                 missing. Always carries a denial reason.
#
# The reason-code space is partitioned into disjoint ranges so the two can
# never be confused and can never be emitted without a reason:
#
#   0          DRAW_IR_V3_REASON_NONE          route == GPU, nothing deviated
#   100..199   policy reasons  -> route MUST be GPU_ROUTE_CPU_SELECTED
#   200..299   denial reasons  -> route MUST be GPU_ROUTE_GPU_FALLBACK
#
# `draw_ir_v3_route_decision_is_consistent` checks that partition, so a
# reason/route mismatch is a detectable defect rather than a silent one. There
# is no code path that produces a non-GPU route with reason 0 — that is what
# "no silent fallback" means mechanically.
#
# SCOPE OF THIS FILE (wave 1)
# ---------------------------
# CPU reference only. No Vulkan, no device submission, no device code. The
# CPU reference is the oracle all three modes must match (shared rule 3); the
# deterministic hash in the receipt below comes from `draw_ir_v3_oracle_hash`
# and is a pure function of the scene, so it is IDENTICAL across
# cpu_reference / hybrid_vector_gpu / resident_gpu (shared rule 5).
#
# INTERPRETATIONS (the plans are silent on these; recorded per lane brief)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Fallback level for a policy CPU route. The L0-L5 hierarchy describes
#    FALLBACKS; a cost-policy CPU route is not one. It reports
#    GPU_FALLBACK_L0_GPU_NATIVE, read as "no level of the fallback hierarchy
#    was entered". The route field, not the level, carries "ran on CPU".
# 2. Level for "no device present" and "mode capability missing". Both deny
#    the whole scene, not a stage or a subtree, so both report
#    GPU_FALLBACK_L4_DOCUMENT_COMPAT (full CPU document render). L5 is
#    reserved for a device that was present and then faulted.
# 3. Capability requirement per mode. The plan's backend tier table (§7,
#    `draw_ir_v3_ports.spl`) is tier 0 = compute passes, tier 1 = indirect
#    dispatch. Read as: hybrid_vector_gpu needs tier 0;
#    resident_gpu needs tier 0 + tier 1. Tier-2 bits stay optional.
# 4. Decision order. Denials are evaluated BEFORE the cost policy so a
#    strict/full-offload profile fails closed on a machine with no device,
#    instead of a small scene reporting a comfortable `cpu_selected` that
#    hides the missing GPU. The one exception is an explicit cpu_reference
#    request, which is checked first — "cpu only must work with no GPU
#    present" (README, Variable execution configuration).
# 5. `accepted` in DrawIrV3SubmitReceipt. Set false on gpu_fallback: the
#    submission was NOT accepted at the requested offload level. This is the
#    mechanical form of §7 rule 9, "no backend reports success after an
#    implicit software fallback". cpu_selected is accepted:true, because
#    nothing was refused.
# 6. Where a capacity verdict is applied. `draw_ir_v3_route_apply_capacity`
#    overrides ONLY the GPU route. A decision that already carries a reason —
#    policy or denial — is returned untouched, for two reasons. A route that
#    already went to the CPU never reserves the GPU output arenas the manifest
#    bounds, so its plan does not exercise those bounds; and rewriting an
#    existing denial would discard the more fundamental one (no device, missing
#    capability) in favour of a later check, making `first_breach_bound`
#    order-dependent. Capacity is therefore an admission check ON the GPU path,
#    evaluated after the selector, not a sixth branch inside it.
#
# BOUNDARIES NOT CROSSED
# ----------------------
# 1. No cross-lane execution profile is adopted here, and the reason is NOT
#    that none is frozen — one is. Read this before proposing the merge again:
#    `StageExecutionProfile` in
#    `src/lib/common/structural/execution/profile_types.spl` IS frozen (§26
#    artifact group 9) and IS numeric — `mode: ExecutionMode` is an enum,
#    budgets and targets are i64, `fallback: StageFallbackPolicy` is an enum.
#    The text-valued `ExecutionProfile` in the sibling `contracts.spl` is a
#    different, unrelated, measured-cost record and is not the candidate.
#
#    The merge is declined on substance instead. `DrawIrV3ExecutionProfile`
#    and `StageExecutionProfile` share no field but `mode`: the §21 record
#    carries deterministic / host+device memory budgets / latency + throughput
#    targets / fallback policy / verification policy / allowed_devices, none
#    of which exist here, and this record carries strict / cost_policy_enabled
#    / cost_threshold_commands / required_capabilities, none of which exist
#    there. `required_capabilities` is the DrawIR backend-TIER bitset (tier 0
#    compute passes, tier 1 indirect dispatch), a different vocabulary from
#    the frozen DeviceMask bits, so it is not substitutable field-for-field.
#    That is the same test §21.1 applied to keep two records apart, applied
#    consistently. `DrawIrV3RouteDecision` is not a profile at all; its
#    counterpart is the receipt, and it cannot be re-typed onto the frozen
#    `StageFallbackReason` because that enum is closed at nine variants and
#    REASON_EMPTY_SCENE and REASON_NO_DEVICE have no variant in it (DeviceLost
#    means present-then-faulted, not never-present). Projecting onto it would
#    approximate a denial reason, which is what this file exists to prevent.
#
#    What IS binding: DRAW_IR_V3_MODE_* 0/1/2 are byte-identical to
#    ExecutionMode.CpuReference/HybridVectorGpu/ResidentGpu and MUST stay so
#    — neither side may renumber. The reason-to-StageFallbackReason mapping,
#    and the rule that a v3 route decision must NOT be projected onto a
#    StageReceipt until the two missing variants are decided, are ratified in
#    doc/04_architecture/compiler/mdsoc/
#    mdsoc_plus_tagged_structural_compute_architecture.md §21.4.
# 2. RESOLVED — this boundary is CROSSED now, and the note is kept so nobody
#    re-derives the old workaround from the old shape of the code.
#
#    It used to read: `gpu_web_capacity_manifest.spl` (frozen) is NOT imported,
#    because that module failed to parse (leading-operator line continuation),
#    so importing it would make this module and its spec unverifiable. The
#    capacity denial was therefore built by `draw_ir_v3_route_capacity_denial`
#    from a bare command count, and the CALLER ran `gpu_web_capacity_verify`
#    and decided what the verdict meant.
#
#    That parse bug is fixed — `69d3e4db82b` added
#    `CoreLexer.leading_op_continues`; the frozen file needed no edit and still
#    has none. Verified at `55115a82411` through the frontend's own gate
#    `parse_module_silent_checked` (the call `lint` makes for PARSE001):
#    PARSE_OK, and PARSE_FAILED again with only
#    `lexer_struct.spl` + `tokens.spl` reverted to `69d3e4db82b^`.
#
#    So the decision came back here. `GpuWebCapacityVerdict` is imported;
#    `draw_ir_v3_route_capacity_denial` takes the verdict and carries
#    `first_breach_bound` into the decision, so the breached bound is now IN
#    the route receipt instead of only in the capacity lane's own verdict; and
#    `draw_ir_v3_route_apply_capacity` interprets `verdict.accepted` here
#    rather than in each caller. "A capacity check that passes must never
#    manufacture a fallback" was a comment addressed to callers; it is now
#    mechanical — an accepted verdict returns the decision unchanged, and
#    `draw_ir_v3_route_decision_is_consistent` rejects a CAPACITY_OVERFLOW
#    decision with an empty `capacity_breach_bound`.
#
#    Still NOT crossed, and not for a parse reason: this module does not call
#    `gpu_web_capacity_verify` itself and does not build a `GpuWebCapacityPlan`.
#    Projecting a scene into a plan is the emit lane's job and it already does
#    it (`draw_ir_v3_capacity_plan` / `draw_ir_v3_verify_capacity` in
#    `draw_ir_v3_emit.spl`). Duplicating it here would create a second, drifting
#    projection of the same frozen contract.

use common.ui.draw_ir_v3.{DrawIrV3Scene, draw_ir_v3_scene_command_count}
use common.ui.draw_ir_v3_oracle.{draw_ir_v3_oracle_hash}
use common.ui.draw_ir_v3_ports.{
    DRAW_IR_V3_CAP_COMPUTE_PASSES,
    DRAW_IR_V3_CAP_INDIRECT_DISPATCH,
    DrawIrV3SubmitReceipt
}
use common.ui.gpu_web_capacity_manifest.{GpuWebCapacityVerdict}
use common.ui.gpu_web_receipt_contract.{
    GPU_FALLBACK_L0_GPU_NATIVE,
    GPU_FALLBACK_L4_DOCUMENT_COMPAT,
    GPU_FALLBACK_L5_DEVICE_RECOVERY,
    GPU_ROUTE_CPU_SELECTED,
    GPU_ROUTE_GPU,
    GPU_ROUTE_GPU_FALLBACK,
    gpu_fallback_is_strict_pass
}

pub val DRAW_IR_V3_ROUTE_SCHEMA_VERSION: text = "simple-draw-ir-v3-execution-route-v1"
pub val DRAW_IR_V3_ROUTE_SCHEMA_ID: u32 = 1u32

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The three modes (README shared rule 5). Same observable results in all three.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

pub val DRAW_IR_V3_MODE_CPU_REFERENCE: u16 = 0u16
pub val DRAW_IR_V3_MODE_HYBRID_VECTOR_GPU: u16 = 1u16
pub val DRAW_IR_V3_MODE_RESIDENT_GPU: u16 = 2u16

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reason codes. Disjoint ranges — see the invariant note at the top of file.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

pub val DRAW_IR_V3_REASON_NONE: u32 = 0u32

# 100..199 — POLICY. The CPU was chosen. Nothing failed.
pub val DRAW_IR_V3_REASON_MODE_IS_CPU_REFERENCE: u32 = 100u32
pub val DRAW_IR_V3_REASON_COST_BELOW_THRESHOLD: u32 = 101u32
pub val DRAW_IR_V3_REASON_EMPTY_SCENE: u32 = 102u32

# 200..299 — DENIAL. The GPU could not take the work.
pub val DRAW_IR_V3_REASON_NO_DEVICE: u32 = 200u32
pub val DRAW_IR_V3_REASON_MISSING_CAPABILITY: u32 = 201u32
pub val DRAW_IR_V3_REASON_CAPACITY_OVERFLOW: u32 = 202u32
pub val DRAW_IR_V3_REASON_DEVICE_FAULT: u32 = 203u32

val _REASON_POLICY_FIRST: u32 = 100u32
val _REASON_POLICY_LAST: u32 = 199u32
val _REASON_DENIAL_FIRST: u32 = 200u32
val _REASON_DENIAL_LAST: u32 = 299u32

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Profile and decision records
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Per-session configuration, not a build variant (README, Variable execution
# configuration). `required_capabilities` is derived from `requested_mode` by
# `draw_ir_v3_mode_required_capabilities` in every constructor below, so a
# profile cannot silently ask for a mode while requiring a different tier.
struct DrawIrV3ExecutionProfile:
    requested_mode: u16
    # Full-offload strict verification: only L0/L1 pass, and a CPU route of
    # any kind (policy or fallback) is not a strict pass.
    strict: bool
    cost_policy_enabled: bool
    # Scenes with strictly fewer commands than this route to CPU by cost
    # policy. 0 disables the threshold without disabling the policy.
    cost_threshold_commands: i64
    required_capabilities: u32

# One route decision. Every field that explains WHY is present; there is no
# "it just went to CPU" state.
struct DrawIrV3RouteDecision:
    route: u16
    executed_mode: u16
    fallback_level: u16
    reason_code: u32
    # Capability bits the profile required and the device did not report.
    # 0 unless reason_code is DRAW_IR_V3_REASON_MISSING_CAPABILITY.
    missing_capabilities: u32
    command_count: i64
    cost_threshold_commands: i64
    strict_pass: bool
    # The frozen `GpuWebCapacityVerdict.first_breach_bound`, verbatim, so the
    # route receipt names the exact manifest field a caller has to raise or the
    # exact input it has to shrink. Empty unless reason_code is
    # DRAW_IR_V3_REASON_CAPACITY_OVERFLOW — and a capacity-overflow decision
    # with an empty bound is inconsistent, see the partition guard below.
    capacity_breach_bound: text

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reason-space predicates
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn draw_ir_v3_reason_is_policy(reason_code: u32) -> bool:
    reason_code >= _REASON_POLICY_FIRST and reason_code <= _REASON_POLICY_LAST

fn draw_ir_v3_reason_is_denial(reason_code: u32) -> bool:
    reason_code >= _REASON_DENIAL_FIRST and reason_code <= _REASON_DENIAL_LAST

fn draw_ir_v3_route_is_cpu_selected(decision: DrawIrV3RouteDecision) -> bool:
    decision.route == GPU_ROUTE_CPU_SELECTED

fn draw_ir_v3_route_is_gpu_fallback(decision: DrawIrV3RouteDecision) -> bool:
    decision.route == GPU_ROUTE_GPU_FALLBACK

# The partition guard. False means the decision is self-contradictory: a route
# without its reason class, or a reason without its route.
fn draw_ir_v3_route_decision_is_consistent(decision: DrawIrV3RouteDecision) -> bool:
    if decision.route == GPU_ROUTE_GPU:
        return decision.reason_code == DRAW_IR_V3_REASON_NONE
    if decision.route == GPU_ROUTE_CPU_SELECTED:
        return draw_ir_v3_reason_is_policy(decision.reason_code)
    if decision.route == GPU_ROUTE_GPU_FALLBACK:
        if not draw_ir_v3_reason_is_denial(decision.reason_code):
            return false
        # A capacity denial must name the bound it breached. An empty bound
        # means the decision was built without a rejecting verdict behind it —
        # exactly the manufactured fallback the capacity lane forbids.
        if decision.reason_code == DRAW_IR_V3_REASON_CAPACITY_OVERFLOW:
            return decision.capacity_breach_bound != ""
        return true
    return false

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Mode capability requirements (interpretation 3)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn draw_ir_v3_mode_required_capabilities(mode: u16) -> u32:
    if mode == DRAW_IR_V3_MODE_RESIDENT_GPU:
        return DRAW_IR_V3_CAP_COMPUTE_PASSES + DRAW_IR_V3_CAP_INDIRECT_DISPATCH
    if mode == DRAW_IR_V3_MODE_HYBRID_VECTOR_GPU:
        return DRAW_IR_V3_CAP_COMPUTE_PASSES
    return 0u32

# Bits required but not present. Subtraction rather than a bitwise NOT because
# `required & available` is always a subset of `required`.
fn draw_ir_v3_missing_capabilities(required: u32, available: u32) -> u32:
    required - (required & available)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Profile constructors — the four named configurations in the plan
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn draw_ir_v3_profile_for_mode(mode: u16,
                               strict: bool,
                               cost_policy_enabled: bool,
                               cost_threshold_commands: i64) -> DrawIrV3ExecutionProfile:
    return DrawIrV3ExecutionProfile(
        requested_mode: mode,
        strict: strict,
        cost_policy_enabled: cost_policy_enabled,
        cost_threshold_commands: cost_threshold_commands,
        required_capabilities: draw_ir_v3_mode_required_capabilities(mode)
    )

# "cpu only" — no GPU dependency; always available.
fn draw_ir_v3_profile_cpu_only() -> DrawIrV3ExecutionProfile:
    draw_ir_v3_profile_for_mode(DRAW_IR_V3_MODE_CPU_REFERENCE, false, false, 0)

# "balanced" — hybrid with CPU recovery and cost policy enabled.
fn draw_ir_v3_profile_balanced(cost_threshold_commands: i64) -> DrawIrV3ExecutionProfile:
    draw_ir_v3_profile_for_mode(
        DRAW_IR_V3_MODE_HYBRID_VECTOR_GPU, false, true, cost_threshold_commands)

# "full offload" — strict resident GPU; L0/L1 only, cost policy off.
fn draw_ir_v3_profile_full_offload() -> DrawIrV3ExecutionProfile:
    draw_ir_v3_profile_for_mode(DRAW_IR_V3_MODE_RESIDENT_GPU, true, false, 0)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Decision constructors
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn _route_strict_pass(profile: DrawIrV3ExecutionProfile,
                      route: u16,
                      fallback_level: u16) -> bool:
    if not profile.strict:
        return true
    if route != GPU_ROUTE_GPU:
        return false
    return gpu_fallback_is_strict_pass(fallback_level)

fn _route_decision(profile: DrawIrV3ExecutionProfile,
                   route: u16,
                   executed_mode: u16,
                   fallback_level: u16,
                   reason_code: u32,
                   missing_capabilities: u32,
                   command_count: i64) -> DrawIrV3RouteDecision:
    return DrawIrV3RouteDecision(
        route: route,
        executed_mode: executed_mode,
        fallback_level: fallback_level,
        reason_code: reason_code,
        missing_capabilities: missing_capabilities,
        command_count: command_count,
        cost_threshold_commands: profile.cost_threshold_commands,
        strict_pass: _route_strict_pass(profile, route, fallback_level),
        capacity_breach_bound: ""
    )

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The selector
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Decision order is fixed and documented (interpretation 4):
#   1. explicit cpu_reference request   -> cpu_selected
#   2. empty scene                      -> cpu_selected
#   3. no device                        -> gpu_fallback
#   4. missing mode capability          -> gpu_fallback
#   5. cost policy under threshold      -> cpu_selected
#   6. otherwise                        -> gpu
fn draw_ir_v3_route_select(profile: DrawIrV3ExecutionProfile,
                           scene: DrawIrV3Scene,
                           device_capabilities: u32,
                           device_available: bool) -> DrawIrV3RouteDecision:
    val command_count = draw_ir_v3_scene_command_count(scene)

    if profile.requested_mode == DRAW_IR_V3_MODE_CPU_REFERENCE:
        return _route_decision(profile, GPU_ROUTE_CPU_SELECTED,
            DRAW_IR_V3_MODE_CPU_REFERENCE, GPU_FALLBACK_L0_GPU_NATIVE,
            DRAW_IR_V3_REASON_MODE_IS_CPU_REFERENCE, 0u32, command_count)

    if command_count <= 0:
        return _route_decision(profile, GPU_ROUTE_CPU_SELECTED,
            DRAW_IR_V3_MODE_CPU_REFERENCE, GPU_FALLBACK_L0_GPU_NATIVE,
            DRAW_IR_V3_REASON_EMPTY_SCENE, 0u32, command_count)

    if not device_available:
        return _route_decision(profile, GPU_ROUTE_GPU_FALLBACK,
            DRAW_IR_V3_MODE_CPU_REFERENCE, GPU_FALLBACK_L4_DOCUMENT_COMPAT,
            DRAW_IR_V3_REASON_NO_DEVICE, 0u32, command_count)

    val missing = draw_ir_v3_missing_capabilities(
        profile.required_capabilities, device_capabilities)
    if missing != 0u32:
        return _route_decision(profile, GPU_ROUTE_GPU_FALLBACK,
            DRAW_IR_V3_MODE_CPU_REFERENCE, GPU_FALLBACK_L4_DOCUMENT_COMPAT,
            DRAW_IR_V3_REASON_MISSING_CAPABILITY, missing, command_count)

    if profile.cost_policy_enabled and command_count < profile.cost_threshold_commands:
        return _route_decision(profile, GPU_ROUTE_CPU_SELECTED,
            DRAW_IR_V3_MODE_CPU_REFERENCE, GPU_FALLBACK_L0_GPU_NATIVE,
            DRAW_IR_V3_REASON_COST_BELOW_THRESHOLD, 0u32, command_count)

    return _route_decision(profile, GPU_ROUTE_GPU, profile.requested_mode,
        GPU_FALLBACK_L0_GPU_NATIVE, DRAW_IR_V3_REASON_NONE, 0u32, command_count)

# A device that was present and then faulted mid-submission. L5, and never a
# strict pass. Kept separate from the selector because it is an outcome of a
# submission, not an admission decision.
fn draw_ir_v3_route_device_fault(profile: DrawIrV3ExecutionProfile,
                                 command_count: i64) -> DrawIrV3RouteDecision:
    _route_decision(profile, GPU_ROUTE_GPU_FALLBACK,
        DRAW_IR_V3_MODE_CPU_REFERENCE, GPU_FALLBACK_L5_DEVICE_RECOVERY,
        DRAW_IR_V3_REASON_DEVICE_FAULT, 0u32, command_count)

# Capacity overflow denial, built FROM the frozen Kernel C verdict. The
# breached bound is copied out of `verdict.first_breach_bound` verbatim, so the
# route receipt and the capacity receipt name the same manifest field.
#
# This is a constructor, not a decision: it does not read `verdict.accepted`.
# Feed it an accepted verdict and `first_breach_bound` is empty, which
# `draw_ir_v3_route_decision_is_consistent` reports as inconsistent — a
# manufactured fallback cannot pass the partition guard. Use
# `draw_ir_v3_route_apply_capacity` for the decision.
fn draw_ir_v3_route_capacity_denial(profile: DrawIrV3ExecutionProfile,
                                    verdict: GpuWebCapacityVerdict,
                                    command_count: i64) -> DrawIrV3RouteDecision:
    var decision = _route_decision(profile, GPU_ROUTE_GPU_FALLBACK,
        DRAW_IR_V3_MODE_CPU_REFERENCE, GPU_FALLBACK_L4_DOCUMENT_COMPAT,
        DRAW_IR_V3_REASON_CAPACITY_OVERFLOW, 0u32, command_count)
    decision.capacity_breach_bound = verdict.first_breach_bound
    return decision

# Apply a Kernel C verdict to a route decision. This is the join between the
# capacity lane and the route lane, and it lives here — not in the caller — so
# that "a capacity check that passes must never manufacture a fallback" is
# mechanical rather than an instruction.
#
# An accepted verdict returns `decision` UNCHANGED. A rejecting verdict
# overrides only the GPU route; a decision that already carries a reason,
# policy or denial, is returned unchanged too (interpretation 6).
fn draw_ir_v3_route_apply_capacity(profile: DrawIrV3ExecutionProfile,
                                   decision: DrawIrV3RouteDecision,
                                   verdict: GpuWebCapacityVerdict) -> DrawIrV3RouteDecision:
    if verdict.accepted:
        return decision
    if decision.route != GPU_ROUTE_GPU:
        return decision
    return draw_ir_v3_route_capacity_denial(profile, verdict, decision.command_count)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Receipts
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn draw_ir_v3_route_name(route: u16) -> text:
    if route == GPU_ROUTE_GPU:
        return "gpu"
    if route == GPU_ROUTE_CPU_SELECTED:
        return "cpu_selected"
    if route == GPU_ROUTE_GPU_FALLBACK:
        return "gpu_fallback"
    return "unknown_route"

fn draw_ir_v3_reason_name(reason_code: u32) -> text:
    if reason_code == DRAW_IR_V3_REASON_NONE:
        return "none"
    if reason_code == DRAW_IR_V3_REASON_MODE_IS_CPU_REFERENCE:
        return "mode_is_cpu_reference"
    if reason_code == DRAW_IR_V3_REASON_COST_BELOW_THRESHOLD:
        return "cost_below_threshold"
    if reason_code == DRAW_IR_V3_REASON_EMPTY_SCENE:
        return "empty_scene"
    if reason_code == DRAW_IR_V3_REASON_NO_DEVICE:
        return "no_device"
    if reason_code == DRAW_IR_V3_REASON_MISSING_CAPABILITY:
        return "missing_capability"
    if reason_code == DRAW_IR_V3_REASON_CAPACITY_OVERFLOW:
        return "capacity_overflow"
    if reason_code == DRAW_IR_V3_REASON_DEVICE_FAULT:
        return "device_fault"
    return "unknown_reason"

# Human-readable reason receipt. Every non-GPU route carries one; this is the
# "no silent fallback" rule in its readable form.
#
# Built by statement-per-clause reassignment rather than one continued
# expression. That was originally forced — leading-operator continuation did
# not parse — and `69d3e4db82b` removed the constraint (boundary note 2), but
# the shape is kept: each optional clause is guarded by its own `if`, which a
# single continued expression cannot express anyway.
fn draw_ir_v3_route_reason_receipt(decision: DrawIrV3RouteDecision) -> text:
    var line = draw_ir_v3_route_name(decision.route)
    line = line + " reason=" + draw_ir_v3_reason_name(decision.reason_code)
    line = line + " level=L" + decision.fallback_level.to_i64().to_text()
    line = line + " commands=" + decision.command_count.to_text()
    if decision.reason_code == DRAW_IR_V3_REASON_MISSING_CAPABILITY:
        line = line + " missing_caps=" + decision.missing_capabilities.to_i64().to_text()
    if decision.reason_code == DRAW_IR_V3_REASON_COST_BELOW_THRESHOLD:
        line = line + " threshold=" + decision.cost_threshold_commands.to_text()
    if decision.reason_code == DRAW_IR_V3_REASON_CAPACITY_OVERFLOW:
        line = line + " bound=" + decision.capacity_breach_bound
    return line

# CPU-reference submit receipt. `deterministic_hash_lo` is the oracle hash of
# the scene, so it is mode-independent: the same scene submitted under
# hybrid_vector_gpu or resident_gpu must produce this same value (shared rule
# 5). `_hi` stays 0 until a backend supplies a 128-bit digest.
#
# `scene_generation` is passed explicitly rather than read from
# `scene.generation`: the scene field is u32 and the frozen receipt field is
# u64, and a u32->u64 widening conversion has no precedent anywhere in
# src/lib. The caller does the widening it can prove is correct.
fn draw_ir_v3_route_cpu_reference_receipt(scene: DrawIrV3Scene,
                                          scene_generation: u64,
                                          decision: DrawIrV3RouteDecision,
                                          elapsed_us: u64) -> DrawIrV3SubmitReceipt:
    return DrawIrV3SubmitReceipt(
        scene_generation: scene_generation,
        accepted: decision.route != GPU_ROUTE_GPU_FALLBACK,
        route: decision.route,
        fallback_level: decision.fallback_level,
        elapsed_us: elapsed_us,
        deterministic_hash_lo: draw_ir_v3_oracle_hash(scene),
        deterministic_hash_hi: 0u64
    )

export DRAW_IR_V3_ROUTE_SCHEMA_VERSION, DRAW_IR_V3_ROUTE_SCHEMA_ID,
    DRAW_IR_V3_MODE_CPU_REFERENCE, DRAW_IR_V3_MODE_HYBRID_VECTOR_GPU,
    DRAW_IR_V3_MODE_RESIDENT_GPU,
